window.__SOC101_PACK__={"courseCode":"SOC101","courseTitle":"Introduction to Sociology","programme":"Nursing","visibility":"programme-restricted","sourceFile":"SOC101_Full_Set.html","credits":["Precious Oladimeji","Bankole Oluwaferanmi"],"notes":{"title":"SOC 101 — Introduction to Sociology","source":"Uploaded SOC101_Full_Set.html. Credits: Precious Oladimeji and Bankole Oluwaferanmi.","topics":[{"title":"Batch 1 — Foundations, paradigms and Marx","sections":[{"type":"html","html":"<h2>1. What is sociology?</h2>\n<p>Sociology is the <strong>scientific study of human action, behaviour, and interaction</strong>, and of how these interrelate to shape social institutions and the social structure that makes up society. Sociology deals with interactions. A working definition covers six linked ideas: action and interaction, relationships, institutions, social institutions, and society.</p>\n<div class=\"box\"><strong>Key distinction:</strong> Psychology studies the individual human mind. Sociology studies people in relation to one another, and the groups, institutions and structures they form.</div>\n<p><strong>Sociology is not a science because we study it.</strong> What makes it a science is the way it is carried out, which is the systematic production of knowledge. Sociology is about reaching the truth through a method, not about the field a person happens to belong to.</p>\n<div class=\"box\"><strong>Exam point:</strong> A person becomes a scientist because of the procedure they follow to attain knowledge, not because of the field they belong to. Sociology claims to be a science because of its method.</div>\n\n<h2>2. Features of sociology as a science</h2>\n<p>Your notes list ten features of sociology as a discipline. Learn them in this order, because a question on \"features\" often asks you to state them and then explain one.</p>\n<ol>\n  <li>Hypothesis formation</li>\n  <li>Hypothesis testing</li>\n  <li>Objectivity</li>\n  <li>Theory formation</li>\n  <li>Prediction</li>\n  <li>Confirmation</li>\n  <li>Observation</li>\n  <li>Conclusion</li>\n  <li>Evidence</li>\n  <li>Experience</li>\n</ol>\n<div class=\"box\"><strong>Exam point:</strong> Objectivity means the sociologist tries to keep personal bias out of the study. Pair it with the ETFOA warning below.</div>\n\n<h2>3. Decisions, bias and the scientific attitude</h2>\n<p>Your notes make a strong point: we should not take decisions based on <strong>ETFOA</strong>: <em>E</em>motions, <em>F</em>eelings, <em>O</em>pinion, and <em>A</em>ssumptions. The scientific attitude is the opposite of this. Looking deeply into things helps us avoid assumptions and stereotypes, understand people and situations more accurately, and avoid reducing a person or group to one story.</p>\n<p><strong>Three habits of good sociological thinking</strong> (from your notes):</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>Look at things from different perspectives</strong> rather than relying on one source of information.</li>\n  <li><strong>Make careful observations</strong> and be willing to consider the complexity of people and situations.</li>\n  <li><strong>Base conclusions on multiple sources, evidence, and a deeper understanding</strong> of the subject.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The three errors to avoid are <strong>stereotyping</strong>, <strong>accepting one story as the complete truth</strong>, and <strong>generalising from limited information</strong>. Your notes reference Chimamanda Ngozi Adichie's talk \"The Danger of a Single Story\" for this idea.</p>\n\n<h2>4. The central concerns of sociology</h2>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>Social order (social statics):</strong> the question is <em>how does society hold together?</em></li>\n  <li><strong>Social change (social dynamics):</strong> the question is <em>how does society evolve, grow, transform, and experience alteration in its basic structure?</em> Anthony Giddens is one of the 21st-century sociologists who discuss this.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Social change is defined as alterations within the basic social structures of society. Those structures are called <strong>institutions</strong>, and the basic ones are: <strong>family, religion, economy, politics, education, and law</strong>.</p>\n\n<h2>5. The three paradigms (the three \"mother lenses\")</h2>\n<p>A paradigm is a perspective that explains how society works. Sociology has three.</p>\n<h3>(a) Conflict paradigm</h3>\n<ul>\n  <li>Explains how society is divided into groups with unequal access to resources, split into the \"haves\" and the \"have-nots\".</li>\n  <li>Key names: <strong>Karl Marx (1818–1883)</strong>, Lewis Coser, Ralph Dahrendorf.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>(b) Consensus paradigm</h3>\n<ul>\n  <li>Sees society as a stable structure that brings order, peace, agreement, equilibrium, and stability.</li>\n  <li>Key names: <strong>Auguste Comte, Émile Durkheim, Herbert Spencer</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>(c) Action paradigm</h3>\n<ul>\n  <li>Explains society in terms of human action, micro-interaction, and the meanings behind those actions. It argues you must understand the actors and their subjective meanings before you can understand society.</li>\n  <li>Key names: <strong>Max Weber</strong>, Harold Garfinkel, Wilhelm Dilthey.</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"box\"><strong>Note the comparison:</strong> Conflict and consensus are often grouped together because both look at society as a <em>structure</em> (system). The action paradigm looks at society through <em>individuals</em> acting.</div>\n\n<h2>6. Karl Marx: conflict theory and dialectics</h2>\n<p><strong>Conflict theory</strong> says society is characterised by conflict between social classes because of unequal access to wealth, resources, power, and opportunities. Classes struggle with one another because their interests differ.</p>\n<h3>Dialectics (thesis, antithesis, synthesis)</h3>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>Thesis:</strong> the existing idea, condition, or social arrangement.</li>\n  <li><strong>Antithesis:</strong> the opposing force or idea that challenges the thesis.</li>\n  <li><strong>Synthesis:</strong> the new condition or arrangement that emerges from the conflict between the two.</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"box\"><strong>Caution:</strong> Thesis–antithesis–synthesis is a model commonly used to explain Marx's ideas. It is not a precise formula that Marx used consistently.</div>\n\n<h3>Social classes in a capitalist society</h3>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>Upper class / Bourgeoisie:</strong> owns and controls the means of production (factories, industries, machinery, businesses), employs workers, and earns profit from the production and sale of goods and services.</li>\n  <li><strong>Lower class / Proletariat:</strong> the working class, which does not own significant means of production. It depends on selling its labour for wages.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h2>7. Capitalism and its characteristics</h2>\n<p><strong>Capitalism</strong> is an economic system in which the means of production (factories, industries, machinery, businesses) are largely privately owned and operated for profit. For Marx, capitalism is defined by the division between the bourgeoisie (who own the means of production) and the proletariat (who sell their labour to earn wages).</p>\n<p><strong>Characteristics:</strong> private ownership of property and the means of production; production of goods and services for profit; existence of social classes; competition among businesses; wage labour; and conflict between capitalists and workers over wages, working conditions, and profits.</p>\n\n<h2>8. Historical stages of society (Marxist sociology)</h2>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>Agrarian or communal society:</strong> people depended on farming, hunting, gathering, and collective activities. Resources were often shared communally, production was mainly for subsistence, and early class divisions were not yet developed.</li>\n  <li><strong>Feudalism:</strong> land was the main source of wealth and power. The two classes were <em>feudal lords</em> (landowners) and <em>serfs</em> (peasants who worked the land and owed labour, rent, or other obligations).</li>\n  <li><strong>Capitalism:</strong> industrialisation, trade, and private ownership expanded. The two classes were the <em>bourgeoisie</em> and the <em>proletariat</em>.</li>\n  <li><strong>Socialism:</strong> a transitional arrangement in which the means of production are brought under social or collective control.</li>\n  <li><strong>Communism:</strong> an envisioned classless society in which the means of production are held in common and class exploitation has been overcome.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h2>9. Auguste Comte's three stages of the human mind</h2>\n<ol>\n  <li><strong>Theological (fictive) stage:</strong> explanations are based on supernatural beliefs.</li>\n  <li><strong>Metaphysical (abstract) stage:</strong> explanations are based on abstract forces and ideas.</li>\n  <li><strong>Positive (scientific) stage:</strong> explanations are based on observation, evidence, and science.</li>\n</ol>\n\n<h2>10. Forces behind the rise of sociology</h2>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>Industrialisation (Industrial Revolution):</strong> technology advanced, leading to industrial production. People moved from rural to industrial settings, a process called <em>urbanisation</em>. The resulting changes were in the nature of social structures, social interaction, and organisation.</li>\n  <li><strong>Ideological revolution:</strong> the Enlightenment and the French Revolution.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h2>11. Practice: theory questions with model answers</h2>\n<div class=\"qbox\">\n  <p><strong>Q1. Distinguish between the conflict paradigm and the consensus paradigm.</strong></p>\n  <p><em>Model answer:</em> The conflict paradigm sees society as divided into groups that compete for unequal access to resources, which creates tension and change. Its key figure is Karl Marx. The consensus paradigm sees society as a stable system held together by shared values and agreement, which brings order, peace, and equilibrium. Its key figures are Durkheim, Spencer, and Comte. Both look at society as a structure rather than as individuals acting.</p>\n</div>\n<div class=\"qbox\">\n  <p><strong>Q2. Explain the thesis–antithesis–synthesis model and give an example from capitalism.</strong></p>\n  <p><em>Model answer:</em> Thesis is the existing arrangement, antithesis is the opposing force that challenges it, and synthesis is the new arrangement that emerges from the conflict. In a capitalist society, the bourgeoisie controls industry while the proletariat provides labour. Conflict between these classes can lead to changes in working conditions, economic arrangements, or the organisation of society.</p>\n</div>\n"}]},{"title":"Batch 2 — Historical stages and the rise of sociology","sections":[{"type":"html","html":"<h2>1. Marx's view of how society changes</h2>\n<p>Karl Marx (1818–1883) was a German philosopher, economist and sociologist. He explained that society changes over time because of two linked things: <strong>changes in the means of production</strong>, and <strong>conflicts between social classes</strong>. Your notes frame this as the historical development of society.</p>\n<div class=\"box\"><strong>Core chain to remember:</strong> change in the means of production → new class relations → class conflict → a new social arrangement. This is the logic behind the dialectic from Batch 1.</div>\n\n<h2>2. Social classes: how Marx distinguished them</h2>\n<p>Marx said social classes are mainly distinguished by their relationship to the <strong>means of production</strong> and their access to economic resources. The same unequal relationship can be described with different terms, depending on the setting:</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>Upper class:</strong> rich, property owners, capitalists, haves, the bourgeoisie.</li>\n  <li><strong>Lower class:</strong> poor, propertyless workers, wage labourers, have-nots, the proletariat.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>These terms illustrate the economic inequalities and class divisions that exist in society.</p>\n\n<h2>3. Capitalism in more depth</h2>\n<p>Capitalism is an economic system in which the means of production (factories, industries, machinery and businesses) are largely privately owned and operated for profit.</p>\n<h3>Key characteristics (from your notes)</h3>\n<ul>\n  <li>Private ownership of property and the means of production.</li>\n  <li>Production of goods and services for profit.</li>\n  <li>Existence of social classes.</li>\n  <li>Competition among businesses.</li>\n  <li>Wage labour, where workers are paid for their work.</li>\n  <li>Conflict between capitalists and workers over wages, working conditions and profits.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>How capitalism developed</h3>\n<p>Capitalism grew out of industrialisation. As production, trade and private ownership expanded, factories, machinery and businesses became the important sources of wealth. Industrialisation also increased the use of machinery and industrial production, and it led to wage employment and conflict between workers and owners over economic interests.</p>\n<div class=\"box\"><strong>Exam point:</strong> Marx argued that class conflict under capitalism could contribute to a transformation of society. His predictions about how societies would actually develop did not fully match what happened historically. Mention this when asked to evaluate Marx.</div>\n\n<h2>4. Feudalism in more depth</h2>\n<p>Feudalism was a social and economic system in which land was the main source of wealth and power.</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>Feudal lords:</strong> landowners who controlled large areas of land.</li>\n  <li><strong>Serfs:</strong> peasants who worked the land and owed labour, rent or other obligations to the landowners.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Characteristics:</strong> land was the major source of wealth; landowners held considerable economic and political power; peasants depended on the land for their livelihood; and conflict arose from unequal ownership of land and the obligations imposed on peasants.</p>\n\n<h2>5. Agrarian or communal society</h2>\n<p>This is the earliest form of society in Marx's scheme, where people depended on farming, hunting, gathering and collective activities for survival.</p>\n<ul>\n  <li>People depended on land and natural resources.</li>\n  <li>Resources were often shared communally.</li>\n  <li>Production was mainly for subsistence (survival, not profit).</li>\n  <li>Social class divisions were not yet well developed.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h2>6. The full sequence of historical stages</h2>\n<p>In Marxist sociology the commonly discussed stages are, in order:</p>\n<ol>\n  <li><strong>Agrarian or communal society:</strong> subsistence, shared resources, few classes.</li>\n  <li><strong>Feudalism:</strong> land is the source of power; lords and serfs.</li>\n  <li><strong>Capitalism:</strong> industry, profit, private ownership; bourgeoisie and proletariat.</li>\n  <li><strong>Socialism:</strong> a transitional arrangement where the means of production are brought under social or collective control.</li>\n  <li><strong>Communism:</strong> an envisioned classless society where the means of production are held in common and class exploitation is overcome.</li>\n</ol>\n<div class=\"box\"><strong>Exam point:</strong> Socialism is the <em>transition</em>. Communism is the <em>end goal</em>. Students often swap these two.</div>\n\n<h2>7. Auguste Comte and the three stages of the human mind</h2>\n<p>Comte argued that human thinking moves through three stages. Your notes list them as:</p>\n<ol>\n  <li><strong>Theological (fictive) stage:</strong> explanations rely on supernatural beliefs and gods.</li>\n  <li><strong>Metaphysical (abstract) stage:</strong> explanations rely on abstract forces, essences and ideas.</li>\n  <li><strong>Positive (scientific) stage:</strong> explanations rely on observation, evidence and scientific method.</li>\n</ol>\n<div class=\"box\"><strong>Exam point:</strong> Comte is the person who coined the word \"sociology\" and founded the field, which is why the consensus paradigm lists him first. His positive stage is the link to sociology as a science.</div>\n\n<h2>8. The forces behind the rise of sociology</h2>\n<p>Two forces gave rise to sociology, and both shaped the questions it asked:</p>\n<h3>(a) Industrialisation (Industrial Revolution)</h3>\n<ul>\n  <li>Technology began to advance, and innovation of technology led to industrialisation. A society starts to industrialise the moment technology begins to advance.</li>\n  <li>People moved from rural settings to industrial settings. This phenomenon is called <strong>urbanisation</strong>. The industrial cities where the machines were set up grew from this movement.</li>\n  <li>Industrialisation led to changes in the nature of social structures, in the nature of social interaction, and in the nature of organisation.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>(b) Ideological revolution</h3>\n<p>This refers to the Enlightenment and the French Revolution. These movements challenged older beliefs and encouraged people to reason about society rather than accept tradition.</p>\n\n<h2>9. Practice: theory questions with model answers</h2>\n<div class=\"qbox\">\n  <p><strong>Q1. Using Marx's view, explain how society changes over time.</strong></p>\n  <p><em>Model answer:</em> Marx argued that society changes because of changes in the means of production and conflicts between social classes. As production develops, for example from farming to factories, new classes emerge, such as the bourgeoisie and the proletariat. The classes have unequal access to economic resources, so they come into conflict over wages, working conditions and profits. This conflict drives the society towards a new arrangement, and Marx expected this process to move society from feudalism to capitalism and eventually to socialism and communism.</p>\n</div>\n<div class=\"qbox\">\n  <p><strong>Q2. Describe the features of Comte's positive stage and explain why it matters for sociology.</strong></p>\n  <p><em>Model answer:</em> In the positive (scientific) stage, explanations are based on observation, evidence and scientific method, rather than on supernatural beliefs or abstract forces. It matters for sociology because it argues that social life can be studied scientifically. This is why sociology treats itself as a science that produces knowledge through a systematic procedure.</p>\n</div>\n"}]},{"title":"Batch 3 — Social institutions and social change","sections":[{"type":"html","html":"<h2>1. The central concerns of sociology</h2>\n<p>Sociology is mainly concerned with two issues, and each has a guiding question.</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>The issue of social order (social statics):</strong> the guiding question is <em>how does society hold together?</em></li>\n  <li><strong>The issue of social change (social dynamics):</strong> the guiding question is <em>how does society evolve, grow, transform, and experience alteration in its basic structure?</em></li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"box\"><strong>Exam point:</strong> Order asks what keeps society stable. Change asks how society moves away from that stability. Students often mix up the two questions, so learn them as a pair.</div>\n\n<h2>2. Social institutions: the building blocks of society</h2>\n<p>Social structures are the systems in society known as <strong>institutions</strong>. Your notes list six basic ones, starting with the most basic:</p>\n<ol>\n  <li><strong>Family</strong></li>\n  <li><strong>Religion</strong></li>\n  <li><strong>Economy</strong></li>\n  <li><strong>Politics</strong></li>\n  <li><strong>Education</strong></li>\n  <li><strong>Law</strong></li>\n</ol>\n<p>An institution is a stable system of norms and roles that organises a basic part of social life. Each one answers a basic need: raising people, giving meaning, producing and sharing goods, governing, passing on knowledge, and settling disputes.</p>\n<div class=\"box\"><strong>Exam point:</strong> Institutions are the structures that change when society changes. When a question asks what social change \"alters\", the answer is the basic social institutions and structures.</div>\n\n<h2>3. Social change: the definition</h2>\n<p>Early sociologists defined social change as <strong>alterations within the basic social structures of society</strong>. This means change is not just a new fashion or a new gadget. It is a change in how the core systems of society are organised.</p>\n<h3>Things that point to social change</h3>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>Technological change:</strong> technology in the sense of tools for survival, and the new tools that transform daily life.</li>\n  <li><strong>Migration:</strong> people moving from rural to industrial settings, a process your notes call urbanisation.</li>\n  <li><strong>Changing social structures:</strong> the nature of the basic institutions shifts.</li>\n  <li><strong>Changing social interaction:</strong> the way people relate to one another changes.</li>\n  <li><strong>Changing organisation:</strong> the way work, groups and communities are organised changes.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h2>4. Industrialisation and its effects (review)</h2>\n<p>Industrialisation brought the changes above. Technology advanced, leading to industrial production. People moved from rural to industrial settings, and the industrial cities grew up around the machines. These changes reshaped the family, religion, economy and the rest of the institutions. This is why sociology became necessary: someone had to study what was happening.</p>\n\n<h2>5. Anthony Giddens and the question of change</h2>\n<p>Your notes name Anthony Giddens as one of the 21st-century sociologists who take the question of social change seriously, the question of how society changes its basic structure over time. Use him in answers about modern social change.</p>\n\n<h2>6. Thinking clearly about society: the scientific attitude</h2>\n<p>This ties back to Batch 1. Good sociological thinking means:</p>\n<ul>\n  <li>Taking several perspectives instead of one source of information.</li>\n  <li>Making careful observations and accounting for the complexity of people and situations.</li>\n  <li>Drawing conclusions only after looking at multiple stories, evidence and a deeper understanding of the subject.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Looking deeply helps us avoid assumptions and stereotypes, understand people more accurately, and avoid reducing someone or something to one source.</p>\n\n<h2>7. Practice: theory questions with model answers</h2>\n<div class=\"qbox\">\n  <p><strong>Q1. What is the difference between the issue of social order and the issue of social change?</strong></p>\n  <p><em>Model answer:</em> The issue of social order asks how society holds together and remains stable. It is sometimes called social statics. The issue of social change asks how society evolves, grows and transforms its basic structure over time, and it is called social dynamics. Order focuses on what keeps things the same, while change focuses on how things move away from that arrangement.</p>\n</div>\n<div class=\"qbox\">\n  <p><strong>Q2. Name the six basic social institutions and explain why they are called the building blocks of society.</strong></p>\n  <p><em>Model answer:</em> The six basic institutions are family, religion, economy, politics, education and law. They are called building blocks because each one organises a basic need of social life, such as raising people, giving meaning, producing and sharing goods, governing, passing on knowledge and resolving disputes. Together they form the structure of society, so changes in them amount to social change.</p>\n</div>\n"}]}]},"cards":[{"front":"What is sociology?","back":"The scientific study of human action, behaviour and interaction, and how they shape social institutions and social structure.","u":"Original lecture flashcards"},{"front":"Why is sociology considered a science?","back":"Because of the systematic procedure it follows to produce knowledge, not because of the field it belongs to.","u":"Original lecture flashcards"},{"front":"Sociology vs psychology","back":"Sociology studies people in relation to one another and to institutions; psychology studies the individual mind.","u":"Original lecture flashcards"},{"front":"How many features of sociology are in your notes?","back":"Ten: hypothesis formation, hypothesis testing, objectivity, theory formation, prediction, confirmation, observation, conclusion, evidence and experience.","u":"Original lecture flashcards"},{"front":"What does ETFOA stand for?","back":"Emotions, Feelings, Opinion, Assumptions. These should not be the basis of decisions.","u":"Original lecture flashcards"},{"front":"The three errors to avoid in sociological thinking","back":"Stereotyping; accepting one story as the complete truth; generalising from limited information.","u":"Original lecture flashcards"},{"front":"Central concern 1 of sociology","back":"Social order (social statics): how does society hold together?","u":"Original lecture flashcards"},{"front":"Central concern 2 of sociology","back":"Social change (social dynamics): how does society evolve, grow and transform?","u":"Original lecture flashcards"},{"front":"The basic social institutions","back":"Family, religion, economy, politics, education and law.","u":"Original lecture flashcards"},{"front":"Conflict paradigm: key idea and name","back":"Society is divided by unequal access to resources, into haves and have-nots. Key figure: Karl Marx.","u":"Original lecture flashcards"},{"front":"Consensus paradigm: key idea and names","back":"Society is a stable system that brings order, peace and equilibrium. Names: Comte, Durkheim, Spencer.","u":"Original lecture flashcards"},{"front":"Action paradigm: key idea and name","back":"Society is explained through human action, micro-interaction and meaning. Key figure: Max Weber.","u":"Original lecture flashcards"},{"front":"Thesis in dialectics","back":"The existing idea, condition or social arrangement.","u":"Original lecture flashcards"},{"front":"Antithesis in dialectics","back":"The opposing force or idea that challenges the thesis.","u":"Original lecture flashcards"},{"front":"Synthesis in dialectics","back":"The new condition or arrangement that emerges from the conflict between thesis and antithesis.","u":"Original lecture flashcards"},{"front":"Bourgeoisie","back":"The class that owns and controls the means of production and earns profit from them.","u":"Original lecture flashcards"},{"front":"Proletariat","back":"The working class that does not own significant means of production and sells its labour for wages.","u":"Original lecture flashcards"},{"front":"Capitalism (Marx's definition)","back":"An economic system where the means of production are privately owned and run for profit, dividing society into bourgeoisie and proletariat.","u":"Original lecture flashcards"},{"front":"Feudalism: the two classes","back":"Feudal lords (landowners) and serfs (peasants who worked the land).","u":"Original lecture flashcards"},{"front":"Comte's three stages of the human mind","back":"Theological (fictive), metaphysical (abstract), positive (scientific).","u":"Original lecture flashcards"},{"front":"Karl Marx's dates and nationality","back":"1818–1883. German philosopher, economist and sociologist.","u":"Original lecture flashcards"},{"front":"What two things does Marx say drive societal change?","back":"Changes in the means of production, and conflicts between social classes.","u":"Original lecture flashcards"},{"front":"Upper class in Marx's terms (other names)","back":"Rich, property owners, capitalists, haves, bourgeoisie.","u":"Original lecture flashcards"},{"front":"Lower class in Marx's terms (other names)","back":"Poor, propertyless workers, wage labourers, have-nots, proletariat.","u":"Original lecture flashcards"},{"front":"Capitalism (Marx's definition)","back":"Economic system where the means of production are privately owned and run for profit.","u":"Original lecture flashcards"},{"front":"Capitalism's key characteristics (list four)","back":"Private ownership, production for profit, social classes, competition, wage labour, class conflict.","u":"Original lecture flashcards"},{"front":"What is urbanisation?","back":"The movement of people from rural settings to industrial settings and cities.","u":"Original lecture flashcards"},{"front":"Feudal lords","back":"Landowners who controlled large areas of land in feudalism.","u":"Original lecture flashcards"},{"front":"Serfs","back":"Peasants who worked the land and owed labour, rent or other obligations to landowners.","u":"Original lecture flashcards"},{"front":"Feudalism: main source of wealth and power","back":"Land.","u":"Original lecture flashcards"},{"front":"Agrarian or communal society: production type","back":"Mainly subsistence, not profit.","u":"Original lecture flashcards"},{"front":"Agrarian or communal society: how resources were held","back":"Often shared communally.","u":"Original lecture flashcards"},{"front":"Stage order in Marxist sociology","back":"Agrarian or communal → feudalism → capitalism → socialism → communism.","u":"Original lecture flashcards"},{"front":"Socialism in Marx's scheme","back":"A transitional arrangement where the means of production are under social or collective control.","u":"Original lecture flashcards"},{"front":"Communism in Marx's scheme","back":"An envisioned classless society where class exploitation has been overcome.","u":"Original lecture flashcards"},{"front":"Comte's theological (fictive) stage","back":"Explanations based on supernatural beliefs.","u":"Original lecture flashcards"},{"front":"Comte's metaphysical (abstract) stage","back":"Explanations based on abstract forces and ideas.","u":"Original lecture flashcards"},{"front":"Comte's positive (scientific) stage","back":"Explanations based on observation, evidence and scientific method.","u":"Original lecture flashcards"},{"front":"The two forces behind the rise of sociology","back":"Industrialisation (Industrial Revolution) and the ideological revolution (Enlightenment, French Revolution).","u":"Original lecture flashcards"},{"front":"What does industrialisation lead to (three changes)?","back":"Changing nature of social structures, of social interaction, and of organisation.","u":"Original lecture flashcards"},{"front":"Issue of social order: guiding question","back":"How does society hold together?","u":"Original lecture flashcards"},{"front":"Issue of social change: guiding question","back":"How does society evolve, grow, transform and alter its basic structure?","u":"Original lecture flashcards"},{"front":"Social statics","back":"Another name for the study of social order.","u":"Original lecture flashcards"},{"front":"Social dynamics","back":"Another name for the study of social change.","u":"Original lecture flashcards"},{"front":"What are social institutions?","back":"The systems in society that organise basic social life (the structures of society).","u":"Original lecture flashcards"},{"front":"The six basic institutions","back":"Family, religion, economy, politics, education, law.","u":"Original lecture flashcards"},{"front":"Which institution comes first in your notes?","back":"Family, the most basic one.","u":"Original lecture flashcards"},{"front":"Early sociologists' definition of social change","back":"Alterations within the basic social structures of society.","u":"Original lecture flashcards"},{"front":"Three things that point to social change (from notes)","back":"Technological change, migration to industrial settings, changes in social structure, interaction and organisation.","u":"Original lecture flashcards"},{"front":"Urbanisation","back":"The movement of people from rural settings to industrial settings and cities.","u":"Original lecture flashcards"},{"front":"Anthony Giddens: relevance in SOC 101","back":"Named as a 21st-century sociologist who discusses how society changes its basic structure.","u":"Original lecture flashcards"},{"front":"Why does sociology study social change?","back":"To understand how society evolves and transforms its basic structures over time.","u":"Original lecture flashcards"},{"front":"What counts as social change?","back":"Changes in the basic institutions and structures, not just small or surface-level changes.","u":"Original lecture flashcards"},{"front":"Good sociological thinking: three habits","back":"Look from several perspectives, observe carefully, draw conclusions from multiple sources and evidence.","u":"Original lecture flashcards"},{"front":"Why look deeply before concluding?","back":"It helps avoid assumptions and stereotypes and understand people more accurately.","u":"Original lecture flashcards"},{"front":"Institution: basic definition","back":"A stable system of norms and roles that organises a basic part of social life.","u":"Original lecture flashcards"},{"front":"Which institution governs a society?","back":"Politics.","u":"Original lecture flashcards"},{"front":"Which institution passes on knowledge?","back":"Education.","u":"Original lecture flashcards"},{"front":"Which institution settles disputes?","back":"Law.","u":"Original lecture flashcards"},{"front":"Which institution produces and shares goods?","back":"Economy.","u":"Original lecture flashcards"},{"front":"Sociology is the scientific study of:","back":"Human action, behaviour and interaction — Human action, behaviour, interaction and how they shape institutions and social structure.","u":"Sociology and scientific method"},{"front":"According to the notes, what makes sociology a science?","back":"The procedure used to attain knowledge — A person is a scientist because of the procedure they follow.","u":"Sociology and scientific method"},{"front":"Which of these is NOT among the ten features of sociology in the notes?","back":"Divination — Divination is not a feature. The others are in the list.","u":"Sociology and scientific method"},{"front":"What does ETFOA stand for?","back":"Emotions, Feelings, Opinion, Assumptions — Decisions should not be based on Emotions, Feelings, Opinion or Assumptions.","u":"Sociology and scientific method"},{"front":"Accepting one story as the complete truth is an example of:","back":"Stereotyping — One of three assumption errors listed in the notes.","u":"Social order and society"},{"front":"The question 'how does society hold together?' is associated with:","back":"Social order — Social order (social statics).","u":"Social order and society"},{"front":"Which of the following is a basic social institution?","back":"Family — The six basic institutions are family, religion, economy, politics, education and law.","u":"Social institutions"},{"front":"The conflict paradigm divides society into:","back":"Haves and have-nots — Based on unequal access to resources.","u":"Sociological paradigms"},{"front":"Which theorist is associated with the conflict paradigm?","back":"Karl Marx — Marx is the key conflict theorist. Durkheim, Spencer and Comte are consensus thinkers.","u":"Sociological paradigms"},{"front":"The consensus paradigm sees society as:","back":"A stable system bringing order and equilibrium — Consensus theorists see stability and agreement.","u":"Sociological paradigms"},{"front":"The action paradigm explains society mainly through:","back":"Human action and the meanings behind it — Max Weber's action paradigm focuses on micro-interaction and meaning.","u":"Sociological paradigms"},{"front":"Which theorist is associated with the action paradigm?","back":"Max Weber — Max Weber is the key name in the action paradigm.","u":"Sociological paradigms"},{"front":"In dialectics, the opposing force that challenges the existing arrangement is:","back":"Antithesis — Thesis, antithesis, synthesis.","u":"Marx and historical stages"},{"front":"The new arrangement that emerges from thesis and antithesis is the:","back":"Synthesis — Synthesis is the outcome of the conflict.","u":"Marx and historical stages"},{"front":"In capitalist society, who owns and controls the means of production?","back":"The bourgeoisie — The bourgeoisie owns factories and industries.","u":"Social order and society"},{"front":"The working class that sells its labour for wages is the:","back":"Proletariat — Proletariat.","u":"Social order and society"},{"front":"Feudalism is dominated by:","back":"Feudal lords and serfs — Landowners (lords) and serf peasants.","u":"Marx and historical stages"},{"front":"Which of the following is the envisioned classless society in Marx's scheme?","back":"Communism — Communism is the classless end point.","u":"Marx and historical stages"},{"front":"In Marx's scheme, socialism is best described as:","back":"A transitional arrangement under collective control — Socialism is the transition. Communism is the end goal.","u":"Marx and historical stages"},{"front":"Which stage is characterised by subsistence and shared resources?","back":"Agrarian or communal — Agrarian or communal societies.","u":"Social order and society"},{"front":"The correct order of Marx's historical stages is:","back":"Agrarian, feudalism, capitalism, socialism, communism — The sequence runs from agrarian or communal to communism.","u":"Marx and historical stages"},{"front":"Comte's theological stage explains things through:","back":"Supernatural beliefs — The theological (fictive) stage.","u":"Auguste Comte"},{"front":"Comte's metaphysical stage explains things through:","back":"Abstract forces and ideas — The metaphysical (abstract) stage.","u":"Auguste Comte"},{"front":"Which of Comte's stages is the basis of sociology as a science?","back":"Positive — The positive (scientific) stage.","u":"Auguste Comte"},{"front":"Comte is credited with:","back":"Coining the word sociology and founding the field — Comte coined sociology.","u":"Auguste Comte"},{"front":"The two forces behind the rise of sociology are:","back":"Industrial revolution and the ideological revolution — Industrialisation and the Enlightenment and French Revolution.","u":"Sociology and scientific method"},{"front":"Which event is part of the ideological revolution?","back":"The French Revolution — The French Revolution and the Enlightenment.","u":"Social order and society"},{"front":"Industrialisation begins, according to the notes, when:","back":"Technology begins to advance — Industrialisation follows the advance of technology.","u":"Rise of sociology and social change"},{"front":"The movement from rural to industrial settings is called:","back":"Urbanisation — Urbanisation.","u":"Rise of sociology and social change"},{"front":"Industrialisation led to changes in all of the following EXCEPT:","back":"The weather — The three changes are in social structures, social interaction and organisation.","u":"Rise of sociology and social change"},{"front":"Which concern does 'social change' cover?","back":"How society evolves, grows and transforms — Social change, or social dynamics.","u":"Rise of sociology and social change"},{"front":"Social statics is another name for:","back":"Social order — Social order.","u":"Social order and society"},{"front":"Social dynamics is another name for:","back":"Social change — Social change.","u":"Social order and society"},{"front":"Early sociologists defined social change as:","back":"Alterations within the basic social structures of society — The definition in the notes.","u":"Rise of sociology and social change"},{"front":"Which sociologist does the notes name as discussing social change in the 21st century?","back":"Anthony Giddens — Anthony Giddens.","u":"Rise of sociology and social change"},{"front":"Which institution produces and shares goods?","back":"Economy — Economy.","u":"Social institutions"},{"front":"Which institution governs society?","back":"Politics — Politics.","u":"Social institutions"},{"front":"Which institution passes on knowledge?","back":"Education — Education.","u":"Social institutions"},{"front":"Which institution settles disputes?","back":"Law — Law.","u":"Social institutions"},{"front":"Which of these points to social change?","back":"Technological change — Technology and migration point to change.","u":"Rise of sociology and social change"},{"front":"Good sociological thinking requires:","back":"Looking at several perspectives — Multiple perspectives, careful observation and evidence.","u":"Social order and society"},{"front":"Why does looking deeply into things help?","back":"It gives a more complete and balanced understanding — Balanced knowledge reduces stereotypes.","u":"Social order and society"},{"front":"Which of these is an error of making assumptions?","back":"Generalising from limited information — Generalising from limited information.","u":"Sociology and scientific method"},{"front":"Which feature involves a sociologist keeping personal bias out of a study?","back":"Objectivity — Objectivity.","u":"Social order and society"},{"front":"Which feature involves testing an idea against reality?","back":"Hypothesis testing — Hypothesis testing.","u":"Social order and society"},{"front":"Which of these is one of the ten features in the notes?","back":"Hypothesis formation — Hypothesis formation is in the list.","u":"Social order and society"},{"front":"The notes say that for Marx, class conflict under capitalism could:","back":"Contribute to a transformation of society — Class conflict could contribute to transformation, though history did not match his predictions exactly.","u":"Marx and historical stages"},{"front":"Marx's predictions about societal development:","back":"Did not fully match actual historical development — The notes say actual development has differed from his predictions.","u":"Marx and historical stages"},{"front":"Which term is an alternative for 'bourgeoisie' in the notes?","back":"Property owners — Property owners and capitalists.","u":"Marx and historical stages"},{"front":"Which alternative term matches 'proletariat'?","back":"Propertyless workers — Propertyless workers, wage labourers, have-nots.","u":"Marx and historical stages"},{"front":"Which alternative term matches 'bourgeoisie' in the notes?","back":"Capitalists — Capitalists and property owners.","u":"Marx and historical stages"},{"front":"Which of these was a characteristic of feudalism?","back":"Land as the main source of wealth — Land was the major source of wealth and power.","u":"Marx and historical stages"},{"front":"Which of these was a characteristic of capitalism?","back":"Private ownership — Private ownership and production for profit.","u":"Marx and historical stages"},{"front":"Which of these was a characteristic of agrarian or communal society?","back":"Subsistence and shared resources — Subsistence and shared resources.","u":"Marx and historical stages"},{"front":"Which of these is the transitional stage in Marxist theory?","back":"Socialism — Socialism is the transition towards communism.","u":"Marx and historical stages"},{"front":"Which of these is the envisioned end of Marx's historical process?","back":"Communism — Communism.","u":"Marx and historical stages"},{"front":"Which of these is a basic institution that organises belief and meaning?","back":"Religion — Religion.","u":"Social institutions"},{"front":"Which concern asks how society changes its basic structure?","back":"Social change — Social change.","u":"Social order and society"},{"front":"The phrase 'social change' is defined by early sociologists as:","back":"Alterations within the basic social structures — Alterations within basic social structures.","u":"Rise of sociology and social change"},{"front":"Which of these is a sign of social change named in the notes?","back":"Technological change — Technological change, migration and structure changes.","u":"Rise of sociology and social change"}],"questions":[{"i":"SOC101-001","c":"SOC101","u":"Sociology and scientific method","s":"mcq","q":"Sociology is the scientific study of:","o":["Individual mental processes","Human action, behaviour and interaction","The physical environment","Economic markets only"],"a":1,"e":"Human action, behaviour, interaction and how they shape institutions and social structure."},{"i":"SOC101-002","c":"SOC101","u":"Sociology and scientific method","s":"mcq","q":"According to the notes, what makes sociology a science?","o":["The field a person belongs to","The procedure used to attain knowledge","Its large number of students","Studying only the present"],"a":1,"e":"A person is a scientist because of the procedure they follow."},{"i":"SOC101-003","c":"SOC101","u":"Sociology and scientific method","s":"mcq","q":"Which of these is NOT among the ten features of sociology in the notes?","o":["Hypothesis testing","Objectivity","Divination","Prediction"],"a":2,"e":"Divination is not a feature. The others are in the list."},{"i":"SOC101-004","c":"SOC101","u":"Sociology and scientific method","s":"mcq","q":"What does ETFOA stand for?","o":["Evidence, Theory, Facts, Observation, Analysis","Emotions, Feelings, Opinion, Assumptions","Experience, Testing, Facts, Outcomes, Answers","Ethics, Traditions, Faith, Order, Authority"],"a":1,"e":"Decisions should not be based on Emotions, Feelings, Opinion or Assumptions."},{"i":"SOC101-005","c":"SOC101","u":"Social order and society","s":"mcq","q":"Accepting one story as the complete truth is an example of:","o":["Objectivity","Stereotyping","Hypothesis testing","Theory formation"],"a":1,"e":"One of three assumption errors listed in the notes."},{"i":"SOC101-006","c":"SOC101","u":"Social order and society","s":"mcq","q":"The question 'how does society hold together?' is associated with:","o":["Social change","Social order","Social mobility","Social media"],"a":1,"e":"Social order (social statics)."},{"i":"SOC101-007","c":"SOC101","u":"Social institutions","s":"mcq","q":"Which of the following is a basic social institution?","o":["Fashion","Family","Entertainment","Sport"],"a":1,"e":"The six basic institutions are family, religion, economy, politics, education and law."},{"i":"SOC101-008","c":"SOC101","u":"Sociological paradigms","s":"mcq","q":"The conflict paradigm divides society into:","o":["Elders and youth","Haves and have-nots","Urban and rural","Literate and illiterate"],"a":1,"e":"Based on unequal access to resources."},{"i":"SOC101-009","c":"SOC101","u":"Sociological paradigms","s":"mcq","q":"Which theorist is associated with the conflict paradigm?","o":["Émile Durkheim","Karl Marx","Herbert Spencer","Auguste Comte"],"a":1,"e":"Marx is the key conflict theorist. Durkheim, Spencer and Comte are consensus thinkers."},{"i":"SOC101-010","c":"SOC101","u":"Sociological paradigms","s":"mcq","q":"The consensus paradigm sees society as:","o":["Constantly in conflict","A stable system bringing order and equilibrium","Made of individuals only","Driven by chance"],"a":1,"e":"Consensus theorists see stability and agreement."},{"i":"SOC101-011","c":"SOC101","u":"Sociological paradigms","s":"mcq","q":"The action paradigm explains society mainly through:","o":["Class struggle","Stable structure","Human action and the meanings behind it","Religious doctrine"],"a":2,"e":"Max Weber's action paradigm focuses on micro-interaction and meaning."},{"i":"SOC101-012","c":"SOC101","u":"Sociological paradigms","s":"mcq","q":"Which theorist is associated with the action paradigm?","o":["Max Weber","Herbert Spencer","Lewis Coser","Auguste Comte"],"a":0,"e":"Max Weber is the key name in the action paradigm."},{"i":"SOC101-013","c":"SOC101","u":"Marx and historical stages","s":"mcq","q":"In dialectics, the opposing force that challenges the existing arrangement is:","o":["Thesis","Antithesis","Synthesis","Equilibrium"],"a":1,"e":"Thesis, antithesis, synthesis."},{"i":"SOC101-014","c":"SOC101","u":"Marx and historical stages","s":"mcq","q":"The new arrangement that emerges from thesis and antithesis is the:","o":["Thesis","Antithesis","Synthesis","Ideology"],"a":2,"e":"Synthesis is the outcome of the conflict."},{"i":"SOC101-015","c":"SOC101","u":"Social order and society","s":"mcq","q":"In capitalist society, who owns and controls the means of production?","o":["The proletariat","The bourgeoisie","The peasants","The serfs"],"a":1,"e":"The bourgeoisie owns factories and industries."},{"i":"SOC101-016","c":"SOC101","u":"Social order and society","s":"mcq","q":"The working class that sells its labour for wages is the:","o":["Bourgeoisie","Proletariat","Feudal lords","Aristocracy"],"a":1,"e":"Proletariat."},{"i":"SOC101-017","c":"SOC101","u":"Marx and historical stages","s":"mcq","q":"Feudalism is dominated by:","o":["Feudal lords and serfs","Bourgeoisie and proletariat","Peasants only","Factory owners"],"a":0,"e":"Landowners (lords) and serf peasants."},{"i":"SOC101-018","c":"SOC101","u":"Marx and historical stages","s":"mcq","q":"Which of the following is the envisioned classless society in Marx's scheme?","o":["Feudalism","Capitalism","Socialism","Communism"],"a":3,"e":"Communism is the classless end point."},{"i":"SOC101-019","c":"SOC101","u":"Marx and historical stages","s":"mcq","q":"In Marx's scheme, socialism is best described as:","o":["A classless society","A transitional arrangement under collective control","A pre-industrial farming system","A religious institution"],"a":1,"e":"Socialism is the transition. Communism is the end goal."},{"i":"SOC101-020","c":"SOC101","u":"Social order and society","s":"mcq","q":"Which stage is characterised by subsistence and shared resources?","o":["Agrarian or communal","Feudalism","Capitalism","Communism"],"a":0,"e":"Agrarian or communal societies."},{"i":"SOC101-021","c":"SOC101","u":"Marx and historical stages","s":"mcq","q":"The correct order of Marx's historical stages is:","o":["Feudalism, agrarian, capitalism, socialism, communism","Agrarian, feudalism, capitalism, socialism, communism","Capitalism, agrarian, feudalism, socialism, communism","Agrarian, capitalism, feudalism, communism, socialism"],"a":1,"e":"The sequence runs from agrarian or communal to communism."},{"i":"SOC101-022","c":"SOC101","u":"Auguste Comte","s":"mcq","q":"Comte's theological stage explains things through:","o":["Scientific observation","Supernatural beliefs","Economic data","Statistics"],"a":1,"e":"The theological (fictive) stage."},{"i":"SOC101-023","c":"SOC101","u":"Auguste Comte","s":"mcq","q":"Comte's metaphysical stage explains things through:","o":["Supernatural beliefs","Abstract forces and ideas","Observation alone","Tradition"],"a":1,"e":"The metaphysical (abstract) stage."},{"i":"SOC101-024","c":"SOC101","u":"Auguste Comte","s":"mcq","q":"Which of Comte's stages is the basis of sociology as a science?","o":["Theological","Metaphysical","Positive","Fictive"],"a":2,"e":"The positive (scientific) stage."},{"i":"SOC101-025","c":"SOC101","u":"Auguste Comte","s":"mcq","q":"Comte is credited with:","o":["Writing the Communist Manifesto","Coining the word sociology and founding the field","Discovering feudalism","Inventing capitalism"],"a":1,"e":"Comte coined sociology."},{"i":"SOC101-026","c":"SOC101","u":"Sociology and scientific method","s":"mcq","q":"The two forces behind the rise of sociology are:","o":["Colonisation and trade","Industrial revolution and the ideological revolution","Religious reform and the printing press","Independence and globalisation"],"a":1,"e":"Industrialisation and the Enlightenment and French Revolution."},{"i":"SOC101-027","c":"SOC101","u":"Social order and society","s":"mcq","q":"Which event is part of the ideological revolution?","o":["The Industrial Revolution","The French Revolution","The Green Revolution","The Digital Revolution"],"a":1,"e":"The French Revolution and the Enlightenment."},{"i":"SOC101-028","c":"SOC101","u":"Rise of sociology and social change","s":"mcq","q":"Industrialisation begins, according to the notes, when:","o":["Trade is banned","Technology begins to advance","Farming ends","Population stops growing"],"a":1,"e":"Industrialisation follows the advance of technology."},{"i":"SOC101-029","c":"SOC101","u":"Rise of sociology and social change","s":"mcq","q":"The movement from rural to industrial settings is called:","o":["Urbanisation","Feudalism","Secularisation","Globalisation"],"a":0,"e":"Urbanisation."},{"i":"SOC101-030","c":"SOC101","u":"Rise of sociology and social change","s":"mcq","q":"Industrialisation led to changes in all of the following EXCEPT:","o":["Social structures","Social interaction","Organisation","The weather"],"a":3,"e":"The three changes are in social structures, social interaction and organisation."},{"i":"SOC101-031","c":"SOC101","u":"Rise of sociology and social change","s":"mcq","q":"Which concern does 'social change' cover?","o":["How society holds together","How society evolves, grows and transforms","Who owns property","Why people sleep"],"a":1,"e":"Social change, or social dynamics."},{"i":"SOC101-032","c":"SOC101","u":"Social order and society","s":"mcq","q":"Social statics is another name for:","o":["Social change","Social order","Social mobility","Social media"],"a":1,"e":"Social order."},{"i":"SOC101-033","c":"SOC101","u":"Social order and society","s":"mcq","q":"Social dynamics is another name for:","o":["Social order","Social change","Social statics","Social class"],"a":1,"e":"Social change."},{"i":"SOC101-034","c":"SOC101","u":"Rise of sociology and social change","s":"mcq","q":"Early sociologists defined social change as:","o":["Changes in fashion","Alterations within the basic social structures of society","Changes in population size only","Changes in the weather"],"a":1,"e":"The definition in the notes."},{"i":"SOC101-035","c":"SOC101","u":"Rise of sociology and social change","s":"mcq","q":"Which sociologist does the notes name as discussing social change in the 21st century?","o":["Karl Marx","Anthony Giddens","Auguste Comte","Max Weber"],"a":1,"e":"Anthony Giddens."},{"i":"SOC101-036","c":"SOC101","u":"Social institutions","s":"mcq","q":"Which institution produces and shares goods?","o":["Religion","Economy","Law","Education"],"a":1,"e":"Economy."},{"i":"SOC101-037","c":"SOC101","u":"Social institutions","s":"mcq","q":"Which institution governs society?","o":["Education","Politics","Family","Religion"],"a":1,"e":"Politics."},{"i":"SOC101-038","c":"SOC101","u":"Social institutions","s":"mcq","q":"Which institution passes on knowledge?","o":["Education","Economy","Law","Family"],"a":0,"e":"Education."},{"i":"SOC101-039","c":"SOC101","u":"Social institutions","s":"mcq","q":"Which institution settles disputes?","o":["Religion","Law","Family","Economy"],"a":1,"e":"Law."},{"i":"SOC101-040","c":"SOC101","u":"Rise of sociology and social change","s":"mcq","q":"Which of these points to social change?","o":["A fixed population","A stable religion","Technological change","A constant economy"],"a":2,"e":"Technology and migration point to change."},{"i":"SOC101-041","c":"SOC101","u":"Social order and society","s":"mcq","q":"Good sociological thinking requires:","o":["Relying on one source","Looking at several perspectives","Following emotions","Accepting first impressions"],"a":1,"e":"Multiple perspectives, careful observation and evidence."},{"i":"SOC101-042","c":"SOC101","u":"Social order and society","s":"mcq","q":"Why does looking deeply into things help?","o":["It makes people quicker","It gives a more complete and balanced understanding","It removes the need for evidence","It lets people ignore other views"],"a":1,"e":"Balanced knowledge reduces stereotypes."},{"i":"SOC101-043","c":"SOC101","u":"Sociology and scientific method","s":"mcq","q":"Which of these is an error of making assumptions?","o":["Objectivity","Generalising from limited information","Hypothesis testing","Observation"],"a":1,"e":"Generalising from limited information."},{"i":"SOC101-044","c":"SOC101","u":"Social order and society","s":"mcq","q":"Which feature involves a sociologist keeping personal bias out of a study?","o":["Prediction","Objectivity","Confirmation","Experience"],"a":1,"e":"Objectivity."},{"i":"SOC101-045","c":"SOC101","u":"Social order and society","s":"mcq","q":"Which feature involves testing an idea against reality?","o":["Hypothesis testing","Theory formation","Conclusion","Evidence"],"a":0,"e":"Hypothesis testing."},{"i":"SOC101-046","c":"SOC101","u":"Social order and society","s":"mcq","q":"Which of these is one of the ten features in the notes?","o":["Hypothesis formation","Astrology","Divination","Fortune-telling"],"a":0,"e":"Hypothesis formation is in the list."},{"i":"SOC101-047","c":"SOC101","u":"Marx and historical stages","s":"mcq","q":"The notes say that for Marx, class conflict under capitalism could:","o":["Stop all change","Contribute to a transformation of society","Remove all wages","End trade"],"a":1,"e":"Class conflict could contribute to transformation, though history did not match his predictions exactly."},{"i":"SOC101-048","c":"SOC101","u":"Marx and historical stages","s":"mcq","q":"Marx's predictions about societal development:","o":["Were exactly confirmed by history","Did not fully match actual historical development","Were never discussed","Were based only on religion"],"a":1,"e":"The notes say actual development has differed from his predictions."},{"i":"SOC101-049","c":"SOC101","u":"Marx and historical stages","s":"mcq","q":"Which term is an alternative for 'bourgeoisie' in the notes?","o":["Wage labourers","Property owners","Serfs","Peasants"],"a":1,"e":"Property owners and capitalists."},{"i":"SOC101-050","c":"SOC101","u":"Marx and historical stages","s":"mcq","q":"Which alternative term matches 'proletariat'?","o":["Haves","Capitalists","Propertyless workers","Feudal lords"],"a":2,"e":"Propertyless workers, wage labourers, have-nots."},{"i":"SOC101-051","c":"SOC101","u":"Marx and historical stages","s":"mcq","q":"Which alternative term matches 'bourgeoisie' in the notes?","o":["Serfs","Capitalists","Wage labourers","Have-nots"],"a":1,"e":"Capitalists and property owners."},{"i":"SOC101-052","c":"SOC101","u":"Marx and historical stages","s":"mcq","q":"Which of these was a characteristic of feudalism?","o":["Land as the main source of wealth","Production mainly for profit","Wage employment","Industrial cities"],"a":0,"e":"Land was the major source of wealth and power."},{"i":"SOC101-053","c":"SOC101","u":"Marx and historical stages","s":"mcq","q":"Which of these was a characteristic of capitalism?","o":["Shared communal resources","Subsistence production","Private ownership","Feudal obligations"],"a":2,"e":"Private ownership and production for profit."},{"i":"SOC101-054","c":"SOC101","u":"Marx and historical stages","s":"mcq","q":"Which of these was a characteristic of agrarian or communal society?","o":["Production for profit","Subsistence and shared resources","Factory machinery","Wage labour"],"a":1,"e":"Subsistence and shared resources."},{"i":"SOC101-055","c":"SOC101","u":"Marx and historical stages","s":"mcq","q":"Which of these is the transitional stage in Marxist theory?","o":["Socialism","Communism","Feudalism","Agrarian"],"a":0,"e":"Socialism is the transition towards communism."},{"i":"SOC101-056","c":"SOC101","u":"Marx and historical stages","s":"mcq","q":"Which of these is the envisioned end of Marx's historical process?","o":["Socialism","Communism","Capitalism","Feudalism"],"a":1,"e":"Communism."},{"i":"SOC101-057","c":"SOC101","u":"Social institutions","s":"mcq","q":"Which of these is a basic institution that organises belief and meaning?","o":["Religion","Politics","Law","Economy"],"a":0,"e":"Religion."},{"i":"SOC101-058","c":"SOC101","u":"Social order and society","s":"mcq","q":"Which concern asks how society changes its basic structure?","o":["Social order","Social change","Social mobility","Social distance"],"a":1,"e":"Social change."},{"i":"SOC101-059","c":"SOC101","u":"Rise of sociology and social change","s":"mcq","q":"The phrase 'social change' is defined by early sociologists as:","o":["Changes in trends","Alterations within the basic social structures","Changes in weather","Changes in hobbies"],"a":1,"e":"Alterations within basic social structures."},{"i":"SOC101-060","c":"SOC101","u":"Rise of sociology and social change","s":"mcq","q":"Which of these is a sign of social change named in the notes?","o":["Technological change","Stable institutions","Constant population","No migration"],"a":0,"e":"Technological change, migration and structure changes."}],"writtenQuestions":[{"question":"Q1. Explain the difference between the conflict paradigm and the consensus paradigm in sociology.","answer":"The conflict paradigm sees society as divided into groups with unequal access to resources (haves and have-nots), and it argues that this inequality produces conflict and change. Its key figure is Karl Marx. The consensus paradigm sees society as a stable system held together by shared values and agreement, producing order, peace and equilibrium. Its key figures are Comte, Durkheim and Spencer. Both look at society as a structure, whereas the action paradigm (Max Weber) explains society through individual action and the meanings people give to it.","kind":"Theory"},{"question":"Q2. Using Marx's views, explain how society changes over time.","answer":"Marx argued that society changes through changes in the means of production and conflicts between social classes. As production develops, new classes emerge: the bourgeoisie, which owns the means of production, and the proletariat, which sells its labour for wages. Their unequal access to economic resources leads to conflict over wages, working conditions and profits. Marx saw this conflict as moving society from agrarian or communal, to feudal, to capitalist, and eventually to socialism and communism. He also noted that actual historical development has not always matched his predictions.","kind":"Theory"},{"question":"Q3. Describe the three stages of the human mind according to Auguste Comte, and explain their relevance to sociology.","answer":"Comte's three stages are the theological (fictive) stage, where explanations rely on supernatural beliefs; the metaphysical (abstract) stage, where explanations rely on abstract forces and ideas; and the positive (scientific) stage, where explanations rely on observation, evidence and scientific method. They are relevant to sociology because Comte, who coined the word sociology, argued that social life should be explained scientifically in the positive stage. This is the basis of sociology's claim to be a science.","kind":"Theory"},{"question":"Q4. Discuss the central concerns of sociology.","answer":"Sociology is mainly concerned with two issues. The first is social order (social statics), which asks how society holds together and remains stable. The second is social change (social dynamics), which asks how society evolves, grows, transforms and alters its basic structure over time. Social change is defined as alterations within the basic social structures, which are the institutions of family, religion, economy, politics, education and law. Both concerns are necessary because a society must both stay stable and be able to adapt.","kind":"Theory"},{"question":"Q5. Explain why the scientific attitude matters in sociology, and identify the common errors it helps to avoid.","answer":"The scientific attitude means looking at things from different perspectives, observing carefully, accounting for the complexity of people and situations, and drawing conclusions only after reviewing multiple sources and evidence. It matters because decisions based on emotions, feelings, opinions and assumptions (ETFOA) are unreliable. The main errors it helps avoid are stereotyping, accepting one story as the complete truth, and generalising from limited information. This is why sociologists are trained to test hypotheses and remain objective.","kind":"Theory"},{"question":"1. What should right science look like?","answer":"Right science looks at things from different perspectives and seeks a complete understanding instead of relying on one source of information. It rests on careful observation and a willingness to consider the complexity of people and situations.","kind":"Lecture-note question"},{"question":"2. What form should a scientific method take?","answer":"It should be a systematic procedure: form a hypothesis, observe, test it, gather evidence, and only then draw a conclusion. The features in the note (hypothesis formation and testing, objectivity, observation, evidence) describe this procedure.","kind":"Lecture-note question"},{"question":"3. What factors should a conclusion be based on?","answer":"A conclusion should rest on multiple stories, evidence and a deeper understanding of the subject, not on one perspective. It should never rest on emotions, feelings, opinion or assumptions (ETFOA).","kind":"Lecture-note question"},{"question":"4. What are the benefits of looking deeply before drawing conclusions?","answer":"Looking deeply helps us avoid assumptions and stereotypes, understand people and situations more accurately, recognise their story, and avoid reducing someone to one source. It gives a more complete and balanced understanding.","kind":"Lecture-note question"}],"counts":{"noteBatches":3,"originalFlashcards":60,"flashcards":120,"mcq":60,"written":9,"totalQuestions":69}};
